# FleetLink Security Standards

## Authentication Flow

```
Login → Server validates credentials → Returns access token (short-lived) + refresh token (long-lived)
API calls → Access token in Authorization header
Access expired → Client refreshes using refresh token → New access token
Refresh expired → Force logout, redirect to login
```

### Token Configuration

| Token | Lifetime | Storage (Mobile) | Storage (Server) |
|-------|----------|------------------|------------------|
| Access | 15 minutes | MMKV (encrypted) | N/A (stateless JWT) |
| Refresh | 7 days | MMKV (encrypted) | Hashed in DB |

- Access token: JWT with `userId`, `role`, `exp`
- Refresh token: opaque random string, hashed with bcrypt before DB storage
- Rotate refresh token on each use (invalidate old, issue new)

## Password Security

```typescript
// Hash on registration/password change
const hashed = await bcrypt.hash(password, 12);

// Verify on login
const isValid = await bcrypt.compare(password, user.password);
```

- bcrypt cost factor: 12
- Never log, return, or store plain-text passwords
- Minimum password requirements enforced in validation schema (8+ chars, complexity)

## JWT Middleware (Server)

```typescript
export async function authenticate(req: Request, _res: Response, next: NextFunction) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.replace('Bearer ', '');
  if (!token) throw new UnauthorizedError('No token provided');

  const payload = verifyAccessToken(token);
  req.user = await userRepository.findById(payload.userId);
  if (!req.user) throw new UnauthorizedError('User not found');

  next();
}
```

- Verify signature, expiration, and issuer
- Attach `req.user` — never trust client-sent user ID in body
- Role checks via `authorize('driver', 'owner')` middleware

## Input Validation & Sanitization

- **Validate everything** at the API boundary with Zod/Joi
- **Sanitize strings**: `trim()`, reject HTML/script in text fields
- **Whitelist** allowed fields in update operations — never spread `req.body` directly into DB update
- **Parameterize** all queries — Mongoose handles this; never build raw query strings

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — mass assignment
await UserModel.findByIdAndUpdate(id, req.body);

// ✅ GOOD — explicit allowed fields
const { name, phone } = req.body;
await userRepository.updateById(id, { name, phone });
```

## Mobile Secure Storage

| Data | Storage | Never use |
|------|---------|-----------|
| Access token | MMKV encrypted | AsyncStorage |
| Refresh token | MMKV encrypted | AsyncStorage |
| User preferences | MMKV | — |
| Passwords | Never stored locally | Any storage |

- Clear all tokens on logout
- Axios interceptor handles token refresh transparently

## Authorization (Server)

- Check ownership in **service layer**: driver can only see their trips, owner only their fleet
- Never rely on client-side role checks alone
- Return 403 Forbidden — not 404 — when resource exists but user lacks access (unless hiding existence is required)

```typescript
async getTrip(tripId: string, userId: string, role: Role) {
  const trip = await tripRepository.findById(tripId);
  if (!trip) throw new NotFoundError('Trip not found');

  if (role === 'driver' && trip.driverId !== userId) {
    throw new ForbiddenError('Access denied');
  }
  return trip;
}
```

## HTTP Security

- CORS: whitelist known origins only
- Rate limit auth endpoints (login, register, refresh)
- Helmet.js for security headers
- No sensitive data in URL query params (tokens, passwords)
- HTTPS only in production

## Error Responses

- Never expose stack traces, DB errors, or internal paths
- Generic message for 500: "Something went wrong"
- Specific messages for 400/401/403/404/409 are acceptable

## Anti-Patterns

| Pattern | Risk |
|---------|------|
| JWT in AsyncStorage | Token theft via device compromise |
| Long-lived access tokens (24h+) | Extended attack window |
| No refresh token rotation | Stolen refresh token valid indefinitely |
| `req.body` spread into DB update | Mass assignment vulnerability |
| Client-only auth checks | Trivial bypass |
| Logging request bodies with passwords | Credential leak in logs |
